<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> choose snowboard pants that are waterproof, roomy enough to squat and layer, and long enough to cover your boots. Insulated pants are simpler for cold days and occasional riders; shell pants are more versatile. Look for reinforced cuffs and knees, since snowboarders spend a lot of time sitting and kneeling in the snow.</p></div>

<h2>Why snowboard pants are a little different</h2>
<p>Snowboarders sit in the snow to strap in, kneel on toeside, and fall more than skiers while learning. Good snowboard pants have a roomy fit for wide stances, durable seat and knees, and wide leg openings that fit over snowboard boots.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Waterproofing</h2>
<p>Because you'll sit on snow regularly, waterproofing matters more for snowboarders than almost anyone. Look for a waterproof membrane and taped seams, especially in the seat. Wet climates call for higher waterproof ratings; dry, cold resorts are more forgiving.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Insulated or shell</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Insulated:</strong> light insulation built in. Warm and simple, ideal for cold resorts and riders who get cold easily.</li>
  <li><strong>Shell:</strong> no insulation. Layer a base layer underneath and adjust for the weather. Better for warm spring days, hiking, and riders who run hot.</li>
</ul>
<p>Legs usually stay warmer than the upper body, so many riders who wear an insulated jacket still prefer shell pants.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Get the fit right</h2>
<p>Try pants on over the base layer you'll ride in, and test the fit in a snowboard stance.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Squat and kneel:</strong> the pants shouldn't pull tight across your knees or seat.</li>
  <li><strong>Length:</strong> the hem should cover the top of your boots and reach the laces or dials without dragging on the ground.</li>
  <li><strong>Boot gaiters:</strong> inner elastic gaiters should fit over your boots to keep snow out.</li>
  <li><strong>Waist adjustment:</strong> adjustable waist tabs or a belt let you dial in fit over different layers.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Men's fit notes</h3>
<p>Men's snowboard pants come in slim, regular, and relaxed or baggy cuts. Regular fits most riders. Relaxed and baggy cuts are popular in the park and give more room to move, but make sure the length isn't so long that you step on the hems.</p>
<h3>Women's fit notes</h3>
<p>Women's pants are cut for different hip, waist, and rise proportions, and they often come in short, regular, and tall inseams. A higher rise or a bib adds warmth and keeps snow out of your waistband. If a style fits your hips but not your waist, adjustable waist tabs help.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Pants or bibs?</h2>
<p>Bibs extend up over your chest with suspenders, so snow can't get in when you fall or sit. They're warmer and popular for powder and park. Pants are simpler and easier for bathroom breaks. See <%= link_to "bibs vs pants", guide_path("bibs-vs-pants") %> for a full comparison.</p>

<h2>Features worth having</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Reinforced cuffs:</strong> tough fabric at the inner ankle resists cuts from your board's edges.</li>
  <li><strong>Thigh vents:</strong> zip open to cool off on warm days or long hikes.</li>
  <li><strong>Pockets:</strong> thigh pockets are easier to reach than hip pockets when you're wearing a jacket.</li>
  <li><strong>Jacket connection:</strong> some pants link to a matching jacket to seal out snow.</li>
  <li><strong>Durable seat and knees:</strong> reinforced or tougher fabric where you sit and kneel.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to wear underneath</h2>
<p>A thin merino or synthetic base layer bottom is enough for most days in insulated pants. Add a midweight base layer for cold days in shell pants. Skip cotton leggings and sweatpants. See <%= link_to "how to choose a base layer", guide_path("best-base-layer-for-skiing") %>.</p>
